\f1\fs22 No cajoling roused him, no performance, no banter over the lyrics he\'92d once gifted them. The Elvi argued over their songs as they used to do, hoping to provoke the lyricist within him. It was to no avail. Chanta lived in a different place, a private, grey country, where all he could picture was Sarah at the top of the stairs, wearing an oversized tee, just as she had the night he first found his voice. 
\f2\i Evolve me tender, Evolve me strong
\f1\i0 , the words hummed in his thoughts, and in reverie he saw the orange glow over Sarah\'92s belly, a supernatural light through which he glimpsed, with aching longing, Little Clinton curled within her. The memory choked him. He realised, with more sorrow than surprise, that not only had Sarah left him behind, but so had her child. He had never considered it before, but perhaps the secret to his creative wellspring had always been Sarah\'92s growing baby, for now, with its loss, he sat barren and spent. He blew his nose on his sleeve and slipped again into a drifting, silent meditation.\
\'93Teacher,\'94 an Elvi called softly. The word echoed to Chanta as if from the lip of a deep cavern. \'93Teacher,\'94 she said again. \'93Won\'92t you sing what you feel\'97let it out.\'94\
Her words dissolved into nonsense. The Elvi, defeated, retreated.\
The next dawn, when sunlight trickled in narrow beams through the ribcage of ruin, and shed gentle warmth across the dust, something shifted in Chanta. Whether it was the Elvi\'92s words at last, or just the passing of days, he needed to sing. With a cracked voice and hands still gripping the stair, he began.\
